codex
Implemented a native Track B generator based on [Strong Identification Codes for Graphs](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.06733).

Key results:

- All local G1–G9 gates pass.
- Structure-aware guesses: 0/200,000.
- Four no-tool attacks: 0/8 each.
- PB-DPLL/CSP reference solver: 8/8.
- Complete affine solver: 8/8.
- Answer: 93 characters, 24 atoms.
- Compact route: at most 250 operations.
- Additional reliability sweep: 40/40 shipping seeds passed.

Deliverables:

- [gen_2108_06733.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/gen_2108_06733.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2108.06733/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

One external blocker remains: every OpenRouter request returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded,” leaving zero valid oracle attempts. Consequently, STEP 4 hardness is not established and the result should not be submitted until the bare and G9 runs are repeated with restored quota. No `REJECTED.md` was written because this is an infrastructure failure, not a failure of G, H, or V.
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index cd1035916e15afa3951301618d14635db6b80f68..e5d2135ae5b4a3c8dbd9767493428cea3f73c063
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 
 The paper has no computational-hardness theorem: Section 2, Theorem 1 is a probabilistic upper bound whose proof directly constructs a code by sampling `Z` and repairing bad vertices; Section 3, Theorem 2 and its auxiliary Lemma 3 are probabilistic existence results for strong-neighborhood graphs. A Track A claim would therefore be unjustified.
 
-For the generated distribution, a complete polynomial algorithm does exist and is disclosed. It exhausts affine images of the base code and runs the exact Definition-1 check on survivors. Across eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 after 17,278,488 modular image operations and 719,937 affine candidates; two local runs took 18.70 and 29.54 seconds under different concurrent load. The compact route notices the repeated directed-difference multiplicity among the marked labels and needs at most 250 exact modular operations. This gap—not NP-hardness—is the Track B claim. Theorem 1's trivial/easy regime also matters: if the graph already has the `r`-strong-neighborhood property, the whole vertex set is a code. The fixed 24-entry requirement and pool constraints prevent that answer.
+The problem-class baseline is pseudo-Boolean DPLL with unit/cardinality propagation and a full-constraint greedy CSP plus legal one-vertex-repair branching heuristic. Across eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 using 4,234,396 packed-wide heuristic operations, estimated as 17,145,069,404 64-bit word operations, plus 55,075,836 DPLL constraint scans in 18.00 seconds total. Its worst case is exponential. A separate algorithm complete for this generated distribution exhausts affine images of the base template in `O(n^2 k)` time before exact checks; it solved 8/8 after 17,278,488 modular image operations and 719,937 candidates in 3.32 seconds total. The compact route notices the repeated directed-difference multiplicity among the marked labels and needs at most 250 scalar modular operations. This compression gap—not NP-hardness—is the Track B claim. Theorem 1's trivial/easy regime also matters: if the graph already has the `r`-strong-neighborhood property, the whole vertex set is a code. The fixed 24-entry requirement and pool constraints prevent that answer.
 
 ## Worked demo
 
